## THE DECISIONS OF THIS ROUND (each Tuesday's default as coordinator; each a Question at the stamp; each a behaviour with a cell)
- **D20 THE BOUND COVERS THE WHOLE REFRESHER CALL** (Q22; Tuesday's ruling 1: "start the refresher off the caller's thread (`Task.Run` or equivalent) so the bound starts at once"). In `RefreshAsync` (`TokenBroker.cs:377`), the call at `:389` becomes a call started on the thread pool, for example `call = Task.Run(() => _refresher.RefreshAsync(key, renewal, user, token))` with `key`, `user` and `token` read into locals BEFORE the lambda (never `state.` or `cancellation.` inside it), so `call.WaitAsync(_refreshTimeout, _time, ...)` at `:390` arms the bound before any refresher code runs. Nothing else of D5 changes: single-flight, acceptance, the late answer disposed (`DisposeWhenDone`, `:497`), the typed reasons. **Shapes this changes and that stay typed (cells, F14):** a refresher whose CALL throws synchronously, and one that returns a null task, must still end `RefreshFailed` with no inner exception (what `Task.Run` makes of a null inner task is UNMEASURED: the cell settles it; reader B1838 measured both shapes `RefreshFailed` at the round-1 head). **Cost, stated (Tuesday asked for it):** one thread-pool hop per refresh (the refresher's synchronous part runs on a pool thread, not the caller's; whether WP1.4's refresher depends on the caller's thread or context is UNMEASURED: that library is not in this lane); a refresher that blocks past the bound still holds its pool thread until it returns (the bound frees the CALLERS, not that thread: a README limit line). **Existing cells that this changes (DERIVED from source; list them by program, Step 3):** every cell that, after starting a refresh without awaiting it, reads `Refresher.Calls`, reads `Clock.LiveTimers` or moves the test clock, now races the pool hop: at least `RefreshCellTests.cs:108` (F05 hang, `Assert.Equal(1, kit.Clock.LiveTimers);`), `:126` (F05 late, the clock moved at once), `:203` (F08, `Calls` read at once) and the `Calls` read inside F09 (`:240` onward). Each first WAITS with the existing `Expect.Eventually` (`Support.cs:357`, the 10 s real-time guard) until the call has entered the refresher and its timer is armed (`kit.Refresher.Calls == 1 && kit.Clock.LiveTimers == 1`), then asserts as before. A cell is never loosened: the assertion stays, only its moment moves; print each changed cell and its old and new line in STATUS. Mutant mSyncBlock (the inline call restored) must turn F11 and F12 red.
- **D21 THE OVERLAP AFTER A TIME-OUT STAYS A NAMED LIMIT** (Q23; Tuesday's ruling 1: "say whether the fix closes it or it stays a limit line, with a cell either way"). D20 does NOT close it: after the bound, the timed-out call may still run (a refresher that ignores cancellation), and the next refresh of that session starts a second call. **What holds, and the cell F13 proves:** while a call is inside its bound there is exactly one (F08, unchanged); a new call can start only after the previous one was refused `RefreshTimedOut` (so at most one more call per `RefreshTimeout` per session); the late answer of the timed-out call is disposed (its bytes read zero), never stored, never announced; the held grant afterwards is the second call's accepted answer or the old one, never the late one; the renewal state the second call receives is the held one (the late call's rotation, if any, is lost: whether Microsoft's library rotates renewal state so that the older one stops working is UNMEASURED). README `## Limits` line. **Alternative (closes it):** the session keeps the timed-out call; while it has not finished, no new call starts (`GetNewTokenAsync` refused `RefreshTimedOut` at once; `GetTokenAsync` hands out the held token while valid on both clocks, else `RefreshTimedOut`). Cost of the alternative: new state and paths in a security library for the delta review, and a refresher that never finishes freezes that session's refresh until the session ends; cost of the default: a misbehaving refresher can have more than one call in flight for one session.
- **D22 THE TWO UNGUARDED CHECKS GET CELLS** (Q24; Tuesday's ruling 2). (a) **F15, the waiter's still-current re-check (`TokenBroker.cs:346`):** an accepted refresh, then an end of the session BEFORE the waiter re-enters the lock; the waiter gets `SessionNotFound` and no bytes. Deterministic form (DERIVED from source: `Raise(events)` at `:432` runs the `NewToken` handlers before `started.TrySetResult(outcome)` at `:433`): a `SessionEvent` handler that, on `NewToken` for this session, calls `SignOut(key)`; the waiting `GetNewTokenAsync` must then end `SessionNotFound`, no `sessions/` name left, the answer's bytes zero, and a second waiter of the same refresh likewise. Under mutant mStaleWaiter (B1839's exact edit, `tests/mutants.py` line 11: the condition made `state!.Id.Length < 0`) the waiter would be handed the cleared grant: the cell must turn red by assertion. (b) **U03 extended, the answer-time subject check (`:446`):** besides what U03 asserts today, the other subject's answer is NEVER written (the fake record store's `Saves` count, `Fakes.cs:104`, is unchanged across the refresh: U03 runs over `new Kit(faulty: true)` for this) and NEVER announced (no `NewToken` event at any moment). Under mutant mSubjAnswer (B1839's exact edit, `mutants.py` line 10: the condition made `answer.Subject.Length < 0`) U03 must turn red by assertion.
- **D23 A HAND-OUT AFTER A REFRESH RE-CHECKS IDLE** (Q25; Tuesday's ruling 3, verbatim: "re-check the idle deadline (both clocks) before handing out after a refresh; a session whose idle deadline passed during the refresh ends `Expired`"). In `ObtainAsync`, in the lock after the await (`:339` onward), directly after the still-current branch (`:346`) and before the hand-out branch (`:350`): when `IdlePassed(state, now wall, now timestamp)` (`:618`, both clocks, the earlier wins) the session is ended with reason `Expired` (record erased, event raised) and the caller gets `SessionExpired`; every waiter of that refresh likewise (the first to re-enter ends it, the rest see it ended: `SessionNotFound` or `SessionExpired`, the kit accepts either for a later waiter and the cell prints which). An answer accepted during the refresh is still written and `NewToken` raised BEFORE the end (D5 unchanged); the end then erases it. **Cell E07** (idle timeout 10 s, the refresher held, the test clock moved 11 s, released: `SessionExpired`, event order `NewToken` then `SignedOut` reason `Expired`, no `sessions/` name, the raw scan clean of both grants; and the same by the monotonic clock alone and by the wall clock alone). RED against the round-1 head (it hands out the token: B1839 measured it). Mutant mIdleAfter (the new branch removed) turns E07 red.
- **D24 A WALL CLOCK SET BACK BEFORE A RESTART: A NAMED LIMIT AND THE BOUND THAT HOLDS** (Q26, a DECISION as Tuesday's ruling 4 asks: "Propose the smallest fix as a DECISION (Question, default, alternative, cost) with a cell; if no fix is sound without a timer or persisted monotonic state, a named limit line in the README with a cell proving the bound that does hold"). **Question:** can the broker, with no timer and no state that survives a restart, notice that the wall clock went back while it ran and keep a restored session's idle life true? **The drafter's reading (DERIVED):** no fix is sound in general. Across a restart only the wall clock exists (round-1 D7); the step happened while the server ran and after the last write, so the record carries only the pre-step wall reading; the only moment the running broker could re-anchor a record is a write, and none need happen between the step and the restart. **Default: README `## Limits` line** ("a wall clock set back while the server runs, followed by a restart inside the restart window, restores a session with more idle time than it really has: at most the size of the step, never more than its idle timeout from its last recorded activity by the wall clock, and only for a step smaller than the time since the record was last written; a larger step drops the record at restart") **and cell E08 proving the bound:** idle timeout 30 min; activity at T; wall clock set back 25 min at T + 1 min; restart at true T + 29 min: restored (the limit, measured as it is); then, with NO call that names the session (a read or `Touch` is activity and would renew it), `Sweep()` at wall idle 29 min 59 s returns 0 and at wall idle 30 min (true T + 55 min) returns 1 with event reason `Expired`; a second row: wall set back 25 min at T + 1 min, restart at true T + 10 min (wall earlier than the last activity): not restored, counted expired, deleted. Mutant mRestoreAnchor (`RegisterRestoredLocked` anchors idle at the restore instant instead of the recorded activity, `:807`) turns E08 red. **Alternative:** re-anchor at every write: each record's last-activity instant written as (wall now minus monotonic time since the activity), and `Sweep` rewrites a live record when the two clocks have drifted more than 1 s since its last write. Cost of the alternative: a store write per live session at each detected step; and it is still open when no write or `Sweep` falls between the step and the restart, so it narrows the limit and does not close it.
- **D25 THE RECORD WRITER CLEARS EVERY BUFFER IT OUTGROWS** (Q27; Tuesday's ruling 5: "a pooled or pre-sized buffer cleared after use, or a bound on those strings ... with a cell that proves no uncleared copy (as B1838 measured it)"). **Default:** a lane-internal `ClearingBufferWriter` (sealed, `IBufferWriter<byte>`, array-backed, no pool) replaces the `ArrayBufferWriter<byte>` of `SessionRecord.cs:69`: when it must grow it copies the written bytes into the new array and clears the old one with `CryptographicOperations.ZeroMemory` before dropping it; `SessionRecord.Write`'s `finally` (`:104` today) clears the live array. It starts at the same estimate as today (`:68`), so the escaped-user rows still grow (the control that the growth path runs). Test reach (internal, as L02's internal accessors are): the writer counts its growths and records, per growth, whether the retired array read all zero after clearing; an internal overload of `SessionRecord.Write` hands the test that report. No static mutable state (L04 must stay green). **Cell P04, B1838's five rows** (user and subject of 1 and 64 ASCII characters, 40 and 60 `+`, 40 of U+00E9 built at run time with `char` arithmetic, never typed): for every row, every retired array read zero; for the `+` and accented rows, at least one growth happened (a row that did not grow proves nothing: the cell asserts it grew); the record bytes still round-trip through `TryRead`. RED against the round-1 head: no clearing writer exists, so the cell is red by its own assertion against a stub of the report that says "not cleared" (show that red; a compile error is not a red: add the internal report type first with a body that reports uncleared, then write the writer). Mutant mGrowNoClear (the growth path stops clearing the old array) turns P04 red. **Alternative A:** pre-size at 6 bytes per string character (B1838's recommendation), proved by allocation as B1838 measured. Cost: rests on how much `Utf8JsonWriter` asks for per string (UNMEASURED), so a future library version could grow the buffer again. **Alternative B:** bound the user and subject length (for example 256 characters). Cost: a new refusal at sign-in for a string D2 does not bound today.
- **D26 CELLS FOR TWO BUILT RESTORE RULES** (Q28; Tuesday's ruling 6, F4). (a) **R08:** a record whose `written` instant is 1 s later than the wall clock at restart while its last activity is not (made as R04 makes its records: read the stored text through `ISecretStore.TryGet`, replace the `"written":<ms>` member by program, `Put` it back) is not restored, counted expired, deleted; the other record restored. Mutant mWritten (B1838's: the `|| record.Written > nowWall` clause of `:749` removed) turns R08 red. (b) **R09:** a record store whose READ fails at restart (`FakeRecordStore.FailLoad`, `Fakes.cs:100`; build the restarting broker BY HAND over a new fake with the flag set before its constructor runs, because `Kit.Restart` makes a new fake and the broker together, DERIVED from `Support.cs` `Open()`): every record counted unreadable and deleted, nothing restored, the store empty after (fail closed, as built: `:789`); README `## Limits` line "a record-store read that fails at restart erases every record it hits: a transient read error at start ends those sessions". Mutant mLoadKeep (`present = true;` at `:789` made `present = false;`: the record skipped and left) turns R09 red.
